WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.232 --> 00:00:14.380
Hi, It's me, Melissa, again.

2
00:00:15.241 --> 00:00:18.664
Today in science class, we started a fun engineering project.

3
00:00:19.685 --> 00:00:23.347
We built towers, using just Jell-O, straws, and cardboard.

4
00:00:24.288 --> 00:00:29.432
Tomorrow we're going to shake our towers to see which ones can withstand the force of earthquakes.

5
00:00:30.493 --> 00:00:33.616
Designing a strong tower was definitely pretty tricky.

6
00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.002
It reminded me of a landmark we learned about in Mr.

7
00:00:37.042 --> 00:00:41.666
Fay's class this week: the Golden Gate Bridge in San Francisco, California.

8
00:00:41.866 --> 00:00:45.129
The Golden Gate is one of the most famous bridges in the world.

9
00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:50.653
But before its construction began, lots of people thought it would be impossible to build.

10
00:00:51.594 --> 00:00:52.875
I'll explain why in a minute.

11
00:00:53.755 --> 00:00:56.978
First I have to tell you a little bit about the city's geography.

12
00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:04.760
San Francisco sits on a small peninsula between the Pacific Ocean and the San Francisco Bay, which are

13
00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.701
connected by the Golden Gate strait.

14
00:01:07.701 --> 00:01:13.443
By the early twentieth century, the city had become very crowded, and city officials wanted to make the

15
00:01:13.503 --> 00:01:16.063
trip across the strait easier for city residents.

16
00:01:17.164 --> 00:01:20.405
For years a ferry service had taken people back and forth.

17
00:01:21.265 --> 00:01:26.407
But by the early twentieth century, the ferries were very crowded, with long lines.

18
00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:31.048
Of course, the obvious solution was to build a suspension bridge across the water.

19
00:01:32.012 --> 00:01:35.773
That's a bridge with a roadway held up by towers and heavy-duty cables.

20
00:01:36.813 --> 00:01:42.615
In San Francisco there was just one problem: the Golden Gate strait is a terrible place to build

21
00:01:42.655 --> 00:01:43.055
a bridge!

22
00:01:44.656 --> 00:01:50.657
Strong winds blow through the strait, and waters flowing down from the mountains collide with the incoming tide,

23
00:01:51.037 --> 00:01:53.558
creating turbulent currents and dense fog.

24
00:01:54.518 --> 00:01:59.500
Plus, the Golden Gate Strait sits close to a major fault line where earthquakes can occur.

25
00:02:00.496 --> 00:02:02.298
It sounds pretty dangerous, right?

26
00:02:03.318 --> 00:02:08.002
Still, by 1916 city officials were determined to forge ahead.

27
00:02:08.983 --> 00:02:13.987
A bridge builder named Joseph Strauss was hired, and he began working with a team of engineers and

28
00:02:14.007 --> 00:02:20.371
designers. His vision was to create a bridge that could handle the harsh conditions in the strait and

29
00:02:20.411 --> 00:02:22.433
yet also serve as an image of beauty.

30
00:02:24.014 --> 00:02:30.349
Construction began in 1933, right in the middle of the Great Depression, when many people were unemployed and

31
00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:31.650
desperate to find work.

32
00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:37.972
Ironworkers from all over the country arrived in San Francisco, hoping to get a job working on the

33
00:02:38.012 --> 00:02:42.554
bridge. Even people just pretending to be ironworkers arrived!

34
00:02:43.494 --> 00:02:48.816
Workers had to deal with the strait's nasty weather, slippery surfaces, and dizzying heights.

35
00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:55.804
On top of all that, they were using dangerous materials like extremely hot rivets and air hammers, which

36
00:02:55.844 --> 00:02:57.726
required special skill to operate.

37
00:02:58.666 --> 00:03:02.269
From the start, Strauss was very concerned about his workers' safety.

38
00:03:02.609 --> 00:03:08.754
The Golden Gate Bridge was the first major construction project where workers wore hard hats, a new invention

39
00:03:08.774 --> 00:03:09.294
at the time.

40
00:03:10.915 --> 00:03:17.200
Strauss also insisted that everyone hook themselves to safety ropes, firing anyone who refused to do so.

41
00:03:18.084 --> 00:03:20.286
And he had a huge net placed under the bridge.

42
00:03:21.166 --> 00:03:28.331
During the four years of construction, 19 workers fell into the net—that's 19 lives that were saved!

43
00:03:29.412 --> 00:03:33.975
However, construction of the Golden Gate Bridge didn't go entirely smoothly.

44
00:03:34.895 --> 00:03:40.519
A trestle being used to access one of the towers was destroyed twice, first by a ship lost

45
00:03:40.579 --> 00:03:42.921
in the fog, and then by strong winds.

46
00:03:43.861 --> 00:03:47.484
And despite all the safety precautions, 11 workers died.

47
00:03:48.364 --> 00:03:51.185
For the first three years, only one man was killed.

48
00:03:52.105 --> 00:03:58.467
Then in February 1937, a large piece of scaffolding fell, killing ten more men.

49
00:04:00.047 --> 00:04:05.389
Bridges are usually painted gray or silver, but the Golden Gate Bridge needed a color that would stand

50
00:04:05.509 --> 00:04:07.310
out in the thick San Francisco fog.

51
00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:09.610
Mr. Fay said the U.S.

52
00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:12.831
Navy wanted to paint the bridge with black and yellow stripes.

53
00:04:13.872 --> 00:04:19.519
Even though that might have looked cool, I think international orange, which was developed just for the bridge,

54
00:04:19.699 --> 00:04:20.440
is perfect.

55
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:26.544
It's visible in the fog, and it looks beautiful against the blue water and surrounding green and brown

56
00:04:26.584 --> 00:04:30.847
hills. By May of 1937, the bridge was complete.

57
00:04:31.768 --> 00:04:38.453
Workers had used more than 75,000,000 kilograms of steel, 129,000 kilometers of cable, and nearly 298,000 cubic meters

58
00:04:38.493 --> 00:04:38.973
of concrete.

59
00:04:43.892 --> 00:04:48.496
At the time the Golden Gate Bridge was the longest and tallest suspension bridge in the world.

60
00:04:50.077 --> 00:04:57.023
On opening day pedestrians, runners, people on unicycles, and even on stilts, showed up to celebrate and travel

61
00:04:57.083 --> 00:04:57.904
across the bridge.

62
00:04:58.945 --> 00:05:02.408
I was surprised to learn that the bridge is still being worked on all the time.

63
00:05:03.289 --> 00:05:06.912
The fog and salty ocean air constantly eat away the bridge's steel.

64
00:05:07.853 --> 00:05:15.330
So a full-time crew of ironworkers and painters work year round, painting exposed areas and replacing corroded steel

65
00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:15.870
and rivets.

66
00:05:16.911 --> 00:05:20.933
Those workers frequently have to walk up the cables, high above the traffic.

67
00:05:21.873 --> 00:05:23.794
I definitely wouldn't want that job!

68
00:05:24.695 --> 00:05:27.016
I would, however, love to visit the bridge.

69
00:05:27.937 --> 00:05:30.918
Several companies offer tours of the bridge and surrounding area.

70
00:05:31.739 --> 00:05:37.522
Tourists can see a statue of Strauss, a giant cross section of suspension cable from the bridge, and

71
00:05:37.582 --> 00:05:41.344
a plaque memorializing the 11 workers who died during its construction.

72
00:05:42.936 --> 00:05:48.638
There are other cool landmarks to see in the area too—including Alcatraz Island, the site of a former

73
00:05:48.678 --> 00:05:56.422
prison. From 1934 to 1963, some of America's most notorious criminals were sent to Alcatraz.

74
00:05:57.382 --> 00:06:03.264
Today tourists can walk around the island, get a glimpse of prison life, and learn about some daring

75
00:06:03.324 --> 00:06:04.205
escape attempts.

76
00:06:05.305 --> 00:06:09.147
To celebrate the bridge's opening, Joseph Strauss, wrote a poem about it.

77
00:06:10.067 --> 00:06:15.877
Mr. Fay recited the poem, and now whenever I see pictures of the majestic bridge spanning the strait,

78
00:06:16.237 --> 00:06:23.140
I think of Strauss' own words: Its great steel arms link shore with shore, Its towers pierce the

79
00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:30.042
sky. Well, I'm sure my Jell-O tower won't last seventy-five years like the steel arms of the Golden

80
00:06:30.062 --> 00:06:30.682
Gate Bridge.

81
00:06:31.522 --> 00:06:34.463
But I hope it will at least survive tomorrow's earthquake test.

82
00:06:35.284 --> 00:06:35.904
Wish me luck!
